# 06-MEA / measurement and economics

**Goes here.** Whether any number can be trusted, and what the numbers mean. Event
contracts, deduplication, match quality, attribution windows and restatement, profit
and contribution formulas, landed cost, return rate, incrementality, consent, UTM
integrity.

## Why this folder comes first

Every ranking the system produces is a function of gross profit per purchase. A
library built on top of broken numbers fills the outcome ledger with garbage that
will mislead the system for years. Collect this before the wedge.

This module also produces the **trust gate**: if gross profit per purchase is not
computable for a brand, the system must refuse to rank anything and name the missing
input rather than ranking on a number it cannot stand behind.

## Not here

- Whether a difference between 2 variants is real -> 09-CRO
- Cash, accounting, tax and how the business is financed -> 20-FIN
- How to install the pixel -> 05-SET
- Retention and repeat rate -> 17-CXP
- Ranking in search, or being cited by an answer engine -> 15-SEO and 16-AEO
- Bot traffic polluting the numbers -> 23-SEC

MEA asks: is the number real. CRO asks: is the difference real. FIN asks: can the
business afford it. SEC asks: is something falsifying it. All 4 fail differently.

**A warning from experience.** 11 SEO and GEO files were dropped into this folder in
one session. Analytics tools and search tools look similar and are not. If the source
is about ranking or citation, it is SEO or AEO. If it is about whether a reported
number is correct, it is MEA.

## The MEA and FIN boundary, which is the one people get wrong

**MEA is per-order economics feeding a decision. FIN is business-level money over
time.**

```
gross profit per purchase                    -> MEA
the landed cost formula                      -> MEA
what counts inside COGS                      -> MEA and FIN, tag both
when COGS is recognised, and under which
  inventory method                           -> FIN
cash conversion cycle                        -> FIN
chargeback reserves and payout timing        -> FIN
```

The middle row matters. Which costs count as COGS, whether freight and duty and
platform fees and returns are inside or outside, IS the gross profit formula the
ranking engine runs on. A source about it should carry `modules: [FIN, MEA]` so the
measurement agents can retrieve it. Tagged `[FIN]` alone, they never will.

